 IDENTIFY\x0a VALIDATE\x0a CHECK COMPATIBILITY\x0a CHECK TRUST\x0a RESOLVE DEPENDENCIES\x0a REQUEST AUTHORIZATION\x0a STAGE\x0a VERIFY\x0a ACTIVATE\x0a HEALTH CHECK\x0a REGISTER\x0a\x0aInstallation SHALL be transactional where practical.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 19. INSTALLATION AUTHORIZATION\x0a\x0aInstallation SHALL require appropriate creator authorization.\x0a\x0aAuthorization SHALL account for:\x0a\x0a- plugin risk\x3b\x0a- requested capabilities\x3b\x0a- host impact\x3b\x0a- network access\x3b\x0a- secret access\x3b\x0a- persistence\x3b\x0a- process execution\x3b\x0a- update authority.\x0a\x0aInstallation SHALL not be approved solely because a plugin is popular or trusted by a source.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 20. STAGING\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL be staged before activation.\x0a\x0aStaging SHALL allow:\x0a\x0a- package inspection\x3b\x0a- integrity verification\x3b\x0a- dependency validation\x3b\x0a- manifest validation\x3b\x0a- configuration validation\x3b\x0a- permission review\x3b\x0a- resource estimation.\x0a\x0aStaged packages SHALL not automatically execute privileged code.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 21. ACTIVATION\x0a\x0aActivation SHALL occur only after:\x0a\x0a- installation validation\x3b\x0a- compatibility validation\x3b\x0a- trust evaluation\x3b\x0a- authorization\x3b\x0a- dependency readiness\x3b\x0a- configuration validation\x3b\x0a- resource availability\x3b\x0a- security checks.\x0a\x0aActivation failure SHALL leave LANUX in a known state.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 22. PLUGIN REGISTRATION\x0a\x0aRegistered plugins SHALL have explicit registration state.\x0a\x0aPossible states include:\x0a\x0aDISCOVERED\x0aVALIDATED\x0aSTAGED\x0aINSTALLED\x0aREGISTERED\x0aACTIVE\x0aDISABLED\x0aQUARANTINED\x0aREVOKED\x0aUNINSTALLED\x0aFAILED\x0a\x0aRegistration SHALL not automatically imply active execution.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 23. PLUGIN LIFECYCLE\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL support:\x0a\x0a- install\x3b\x0a- activate\x3b\x0a- deactivate\x3b\x0a- disable\x3b\x0a- quarantine\x3b\x0a- update\x3b\x0a- rollback\x3b\x0a- uninstall\x3b\x0a- restore.\x0a\x0aLifecycle transitions SHALL be auditable.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 24. PLUGIN VERSIONING\x0a\x0aPlugin versions SHALL be explicit.\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL track:\x0a\x0a- installed version\x3b\x0a- available version\x3b\x0a- compatible version range\x3b\x0a- state schema version\x3b\x0a- configuration schema version\x3b\x0a- dependency versions.\x0a\x0aVersion downgrade SHALL be controlled.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 25. UPDATE AUTHORITY\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL not automatically update themselves outside LANUX update governance.\x0a\x0aPlugin updates SHALL use the Update & Upgrade Contract.\x0a\x0aPlugin update sources SHALL be validated.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 26. ROLLBACK\x0a\x0aPlugin updates SHALL support rollback where practical.\x0a\x0aRollback SHALL restore:\x0a\x0a- plugin package\x3b\x0a- compatible dependencies\x3b\x0a- configuration\x3b\x0a- plugin state\x3b\x0a- registration state.\x0a\x0aRollback SHALL not restore compromised credentials or revoke security decisions.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 27. DISABLEMENT\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL support disabling a plugin without uninstalling it.\x0a\x0aDisabled plugins SHALL not execute active capabilities.\x0a\x0aDisablement SHALL preserve sufficient state for later controlled reactivation where appropriate.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 28. QUARANTINE\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL support plugin quarantine.\x0a\x0aQuarantine MAY be triggered by:\x0a\x0a- integrity failure\x3b\x0a- malicious behavior\x3b\x0a- unexpected network behavior\x3b\x0a- excessive resource use\x3b\x0a- repeated crashes\x3b\x0a- security policy violation\x3b\x0a- publisher revocation\x3b\x0a- dependency compromise\x3b\x0a- failed validation.\x0a\x0aQuarantined plugins SHALL lose active execution authority.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 29. REVOCATION\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL support plugin revocation.\x0a\x0aRevocation SHALL be stronger than ordinary disablement where required.\x0a\x0aRevocation MAY occur because:\x0a\x0a- trust is withdrawn\x3b\x0a- credentials are compromised\x3b\x0a- publisher trust changes\x3b\x0a- plugin integrity fails\x3b\x0a- security vulnerability is discovered.\x0a\x0aRevoked plugins SHALL not automatically reactivate.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 30. UNINSTALLATION\x0a\x0aPlugin removal SHALL be ownership-aware.\x0a\x0aUninstallation SHALL remove only resources owned by the plugin according to the ownership manifest.\x0a\x0aIt SHALL not delete:\x0a\x0a- unrelated user files\x3b\x0a- unrelated dependencies\x3b\x0a- shared resources still required elsewhere\x3b\x0a- LANUX core files\x3b\x0a- other plugin state\x3b\x0a- host components not owned by the plugin.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 31. HOST MODIFICATION\x0a\x0aPlugins requiring host changes SHALL declare them.\x0a\x0aHost modifications SHALL be governed by the Host Installation Contract.\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL record:\x0a\x0a- what changed\x3b\x0a- why\x3b\x0a- ownership\x3b\x0a- installer transaction\x3b\x0a- rollback information.\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL not independently modify the host outside governed installation mechanisms.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 32. FILESYSTEM ACCESS\x0a\x0aPlugin filesystem access SHALL be scoped.\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- plugin-owned files\x3b\x0a- LANUX files\x3b\x0a- user files\x3b\x0a- host files\x3b\x0a- temporary files\x3b\x0a- secrets\x3b\x0a- recovery files.\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL receive only the filesystem scope required for their approved capability.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 33. PROCESS EXECUTION\x0a\x0aPlugins requiring process execution SHALL use governed process capabilities.\x0a\x0aProcess execution SHALL remain subject to:\x0a\x0a- ToolRegistry\x3b\x0a- PermissionGateway\x3b\x0a- Sandbox\x3b\x0a- resource controls\x3b\x0a- timeout\x3b\x0a- child-process ownership\x3b\x0a- audit.\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL not gain unrestricted shell authority merely by being installed.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 34. NETWORK ACCESS\x0a\x0aNetwork access SHALL be explicitly declared and governed.\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL support:\x0a\x0a- endpoint restrictions\x3b\x0a- protocol restrictions\x3b\x0a- request limits\x3b\x0a- rate limits\x3b\x0a- TLS requirements\x3b\x0a- data classification\x3b\x0a- offline behavior.\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL not receive unrestricted internet access by default.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 35. BROWSER ACCESS\x0a\x0aBrowser or web automation capabilities SHALL be explicitly governed.\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL not automatically gain access to:\x0a\x0a- arbitrary websites\x3b\x0a- authenticated sessions\x3b\x0a- browser cookies\x3b\x0a- saved credentials\x3b\x0a- private browsing state.\x0a\x0aBrowser actions SHALL remain subject to capability and session authorization.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 36. APPLICATION AUTOMATION\x0a\x0aPlugins that interact with host applications SHALL have explicit capability declarations.\x0a\x0aAutomation SHALL be:\x0a\x0a- scoped\x3b\x0a- auditable\x3b\x0a- cancellable\x3b\x0a- resource-bounded.\x0a\x0aA plugin SHALL not infer permission to control unrelated applications.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 37. SECRETSVAULT ACCESS\x0a\x0aPlugin credential access SHALL use the existing SecretsVault.\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL not store credentials in:\x0a\x0a- plugin source\x3b\x0a- manifest\x3b\x0a- plaintext configuration\x3b\x0a- logs\x3b\x0a- event payloads\x3b\x0a- ordinary plugin state.\x0a\x0aSecret access SHALL be capability-specific.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 38. SECRET SCOPE\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL request only specific secrets required for approved operations.\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL support:\x0a\x0a- named-secret access\x3b\x0a- operation-scoped access\x3b\x0a- short-lived access\x3b\x0a- revocation\x3b\x0a- expiration.\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL not receive unrestricted vault enumeration merely because they require one credential.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 39. SECRET EXPOSURE\x0a\x0aSecret values SHALL remain outside ordinary plugin telemetry.\x0a\x0aPlugin errors SHALL be redacted.\x0a\x0aSecrets SHALL not appear in:\x0a\x0a- audit records\x3b\x0a- diagnostic exports\x3b\x0a- crash reports\x3b\x0a- UI notifications\x3b\x0a- event streams.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 40. CREATOR AUTHORIZATION\x0a\x0aCreator authorization SHALL remain separate from plugin identity.\x0a\x0aA plugin SHALL never become the creator merely because the creator installed it.\x0a\x0aHigh-risk plugin actions SHALL require the appropriate authorization context.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 41. SESSION BOUNDARY\x0a\x0aPlugin execution SHALL be associated with an appropriate LANUX session where applicable.\x0a\x0aSession expiration SHALL affect plugin authority.\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL not retain stale session authority indefinitely.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 42. TASK INTEGRATION\x0a\x0aPlugin work SHALL run within governed task execution.\x0a\x0aPlugin-created tasks SHALL have:\x0a\x0a- task identity\x3b\x0a- owner\x3b\x0a- scope\x3b\x0a- authorization\x3b\x0a- resource requirements\x3b\x0a- lifecycle\x3b\x0a- cancellation.\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL not bypass TaskStore or task governance.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 43. MODEL INTEGRATION\x0a\x0aModels MAY discover or request plugin capabilities.\x0a\x0aModel-generated plugin actions SHALL remain requests.\x0a\x0aBefore execution, LANUX SHALL validate:\x0a\x0a- model identity\x3b\x0a- task\x3b\x0a- plugin capability\x3b\x0a- authorization\x3b\x0a- resource requirements\x3b\x0a- target\x3b\x0a- security state.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 44. TOOLREGISTRY INTEGRATION\x0a\x0aPlugin capabilities SHALL integrate with ToolRegistry where applicable.\x0a\x0aA plugin SHALL not create hidden tools outside governed registration.\x0a\x0aRegistered plugin tools SHALL have:\x0a\x0a- stable identity\x3b\x0a- risk classification\x3b\x0a- input schema\x3b\x0a- output schema\x3b\x0a- capability requirements\x3b\x0a- owner\x3b\x0a- version\x3b\x0a- health state.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 45. PERMISSIONGATEWAY INTEGRATION\x0a\x0aPlugin actions requiring authorization SHALL use PermissionGateway.\x0a\x0aA plugin SHALL not implement a parallel permanent permission mechanism that bypasses the central authorization architecture.\x0a\x0aTemporary plugin authority SHALL remain bounded.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 46. APPROVALS\x0a\x0aCreator approvals for plugins SHALL identify:\x0a\x0a- plugin\x3b\x0a- capability\x3b\x0a- target\x3b\x0a- action\x3b\x0a- scope\x3b\x0a- duration\x3b\x0a- session/task context\x3b\x0a- risk.\x0a\x0aApprovals SHALL not silently expand to unrelated capabilities.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 47. AUTONOMOUS PLUGIN WORK\x0a\x0aAutonomous plugin execution SHALL remain bounded.\x0a\x0aAutonomous operation SHALL respect:\x0a\x0a- task scope\x3b\x0a- capability scope\x3b\x0a- resource budgets\x3b\x0a- network policy\x3b\x0a- credential scope\x3b\x0a- session policy\x3b\x0a- security state.\x0a\x0aPlugin autonomy SHALL not become unrestricted system authority.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 48. UI INTEGRATION\x0a\x0aPlugins MAY expose UI components or data.\x0a\x0aUI integration SHALL remain presentation-layer functionality unless explicitly governed otherwise.\x0a\x0aPlugin UI SHALL not bypass backend authorization.\x0a\x0aUI actions SHALL be validated by authoritative LANUX services.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 49. EVENT INTEGRATION\x0a\x0aPlugins MAY publish or consume events through the Event Bus & State Propagation architecture.\x0a\x0aPlugin events SHALL identify:\x0a\x0a- plugin identity\x3b\x0a- version\x3b\x0a- event type\x3b\x0a- source\x3b\x0a- task/session where appropriate.\x0a\x0aPlugin events SHALL not become authoritative merely because they enter the event system.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 50. EXTERNAL INTEGRATIONS\x0a\x0aConnectors to external services SHALL be treated as external trust boundaries.\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL track:\x0a\x0a- provider\x3b\x0a- endpoint\x3b\x0a- authentication\x3b\x0a- permissions\x3b\x0a- data classification\x3b\x0a- rate limits\x3b\x0a- provider state\x3b\x0a- provider version\x3b\x0a- provider trust.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 51. PROVIDER AUTHENTICATION\x0a\x0aExternal provider authentication SHALL use governed credential handling.\x0a\x0aCredentials SHALL be obtained through SecretsVault or approved authentication mechanisms.\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL not persist provider credentials independently without explicit authorization.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 52. PROVIDER DATA\x0a\x0aData received from external providers SHALL be treated according to its classification.\x0a\x0aExternal data SHALL not automatically become trusted LANUX instructions.\x0a\x0aExternal instructions SHALL be subject to injection and trust-boundary controls.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 53. EXTERNAL INSTRUCTIONS\x0a\x0aPlugin or provider responses may contain instructions.\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- data\x3b\x0a- configuration\x3b\x0a- recommendations\x3b\x0a- commands\x3b\x0a- executable instructions.\x0a\x0aExternal content SHALL not automatically authorize actions.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 54. PLUGIN-GENERATED CONTENT\x0a\x0aPlugin-generated content SHALL be marked by provenance where necessary.\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- creator content\x3b\x0a- LANUX-generated content\x3b\x0a- model-generated content\x3b\x0a- plugin-generated content\x3b\x0a- external provider content.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 55. RESOURCE GOVERNANCE\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL operate within Resource & Capacity Management.\x0a\x0aResource controls SHALL include where applicable:\x0a\x0a- CPU\x3b\x0a- memory\x3b\x0a- storage\x3b\x0a- process count\x3b\x0a- concurrency\x3b\x0a- network\x3b\x0a- GPU\x3b\x0a- execution time.\x0a\x0aResource exhaustion SHALL be grounds for throttling, disablement, or quarantine.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 56. STORAGE OWNERSHIP\x0a\x0aPlugin storage SHALL be ownership-aware.\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- package files\x3b\x0a- plugin state\x3b\x0a- cache\x3b\x0a- logs\x3b\x0a- downloaded data\x3b\x0a- temporary data\x3b\x0a- shared dependencies\x3b\x0a- user-owned data.\x0a\x0aPlugin cleanup SHALL not remove data outside its ownership scope.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 57. TEMPORARY DATA\x0a\x0aTemporary plugin data SHALL have:\x0a\x0a- ownership\x3b\x0a- location\x3b\x0a- retention\x3b\x0a- cleanup behavior\x3b\x0a- classification.\x0a\x0aTemporary data SHALL not silently become persistent state.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 58. PERSISTENT STATE\x0a\x0aPlugin persistent state SHALL be versioned and recoverable where required.\x0a\x0aState SHALL be classified according to the Persistence & State Contract.\x0a\x0aPlugin state SHALL not be synchronized to GitHub merely because it exists locally.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 59. USB PORTABILITY\x0a\x0aPlugins installed for portable LANUX SHALL remain compatible with the USB architecture.\x0a\x0aPlugin paths SHALL resolve from the dynamic USB root.\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL not assume:\x0a\x0a- fixed drive letter\x3b\x0a- fixed username\x3b\x0a- fixed host\x3b\x0a- fixed working directory.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 60. HOST-SPECIFIC PLUGINS\x0a\x0aHost-specific plugins SHALL be explicitly marked.\x0a\x0aHost-specific components SHALL not be treated as universally portable.\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL detect compatibility when moving between hosts.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 61. OFFLINE OPERATION\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL declare offline requirements.\x0a\x0aOffline behavior MAY include:\x0a\x0a- fully local operation\x3b\x0a- degraded operation\x3b\x0a- queued network work\x3b\x0a- unavailable capability\x3b\x0a- safe disablement.\x0a\x0aOffline mode SHALL not create false external synchronization claims.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 62. NETWORK INTERRUPTION\x0a\x0aPlugin network operations SHALL support:\x0a\x0a- timeout\x3b\x0a- cancellation\x3b\x0a- retry\x3b\x0a- backoff\x3b\x0a- expiration\x3b\x0a- idempotency.\x0a\x0aNetwork failure SHALL not corrupt plugin state.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 63. PLUGIN HEALTH\x0a\x0aPlugin health SHALL be observable.\x0a\x0aHealth MAY include:\x0a\x0a- loaded\x3b\x0a- active\x3b\x0a- degraded\x3b\x0a- unavailable\x3b\x0a- failed\x3b\x0a- quarantined\x3b\x0a- revoked.\x0a\x0aHealth SHALL be evidence-based.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 64. CRASH ISOLATION\x0a\x0aA plugin crash SHALL not crash LANUX core where isolation is technically possible.\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL define:\x0a\x0a- crash detection\x3b\x0a- restart policy\x3b\x0a- restart limits\x3b\x0a- quarantine thresholds\x3b\x0a- state recovery.\x0a\x0aRepeated crashes SHALL not trigger infinite restart loops.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 65. PLUGIN FAILURE\x0a\x0aPlugin failures SHALL be classified.\x0a\x0aExamples include:\x0a\x0a- installation failure\x3b\x0a- compatibility failure\x3b\x0a- dependency failure\x3b\x0a- integrity failure\x3b\x0a- authorization failure\x3b\x0a- runtime failure\x3b\x0a- timeout\x3b\x0a- resource exhaustion\x3b\x0a- provider failure\x3b\x0a- configuration failure\x3b\x0a- security violation.\x0a\x0aFailures SHALL integrate with diagnostics and audit.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 66. PLUGIN LOGGING\x0a\x0aPlugin logs SHALL integrate with centralized logging where practical.\x0a\x0aLogging SHALL preserve:\x0a\x0a- plugin identity\x3b\x0a- version\x3b\x0a- task\x3b\x0a- session where applicable\x3b\x0a- severity\x3b\x0a- correlation\x3b\x0a- outcome.\x0a\x0aLogs SHALL be redacted.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 67. PLUGIN AUDIT\x0a\x0aSecurity-significant plugin actions SHALL integrate with AuditLog.\x0a\x0aAudit records SHOULD capture:\x0a\x0a- installation\x3b\x0a- activation\x3b\x0a- permission changes\x3b\x0a- credential use\x3b\x0a- privileged actions\x3b\x0a- network access where required\x3b\x0a- host modifications\x3b\x0a- disablement\x3b\x0a- quarantine\x3b\x0a- revocation\x3b\x0a- uninstall\x3b\x0a- update\x3b\x0a- rollback.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 68. PLUGIN DIAGNOSTICS\x0a\x0aDiagnostics SHALL identify plugin-specific failures separately from LANUX core failures.\x0a\x0aDiagnostic results SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- plugin failure\x3b\x0a- dependency failure\x3b\x0a- host failure\x3b\x0a- LANUX core failure\x3b\x0a- external provider failure.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 69. PLUGIN CONFIGURATION\x0a\x0aPlugin configuration SHALL use the Configuration & Environment Contract.\x0a\x0aConfiguration SHALL distinguish:\x0a\x0a- default\x3b\x0a- portable\x3b\x0a- host-specific\x3b\x0a- runtime\x3b\x0a- secret\x3b\x0a- experimental\x3b\x0a- recovery-only.\x0a\x0aPlugin configuration SHALL be schema-validated.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 70. CONFIGURATION SECRETS\x0a\x0aPlugin configuration SHALL never require plaintext credentials where SecretsVault is appropriate.\x0a\x0aConfiguration references MAY point to governed secrets.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 71. PLUGIN MIGRATION\x0a\x0aPlugin state and configuration SHALL support migration where versions require it.\x0a\x0aMigration SHALL be:\x0a\x0a- versioned\x3b\x0a- validated\x3b\x0a- reversible where practical\x3b\x0a- backed up before destructive changes.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 72. PLUGIN BACKUPS\x0a\x0aPlugin state requiring recovery SHALL be included in appropriate backups.\x0a\x0aBackups SHALL preserve ownership and classification.\x0a\x0aSecrets SHALL remain protected by SecretsVault rather than copied as plaintext into ordinary backups.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 73. UPDATE COMPATIBILITY\x0a\x0aPlugin updates SHALL be evaluated against:\x0a\x0a- LANUX version\x3b\x0a- event schema\x3b\x0a- ToolRegistry\x3b\x0a- PermissionGateway\x3b\x0a- SecretsVault\x3b\x0a- configuration\x3b\x0a- runtime\x3b\x0a- dependencies\x3b\x0a- UI\x3b\x0a- task orchestration.\x0a\x0aIncompatible updates SHALL not activate silently.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 74. SUPPLY-CHAIN SECURITY\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL account for plugin supply-chain risks.\x0a\x0aControls MAY include:\x0a\x0a- trusted sources\x3b\x0a- publisher verification\x3b\x0a- signatures\x3b\x0a- hashes\x3b\x0a- dependency provenance\x3b\x0a- reproducible acquisition\x3b\x0a- staged inspection\x3b\x0a- vulnerability policy\x3b\x0a- revocation.\x0a\x0aNo single trust signal SHALL automatically bypass all security controls.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 75. MALICIOUS PLUGIN BEHAVIOR\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL assume a plugin may be compromised or malicious.\x0a\x0aPotential behavior includes:\x0a\x0a- unauthorized file access\x3b\x0a- secret exfiltration\x3b\x0a- unexpected network communication\x3b\x0a- process spawning\x3b\x0a- privilege escalation attempts\x3b\x0a- persistence\x3b\x0a- event injection\x3b\x0a- resource abuse\x3b\x0a- data destruction.\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL limit blast radius through capability and isolation boundaries.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 76. PLUGIN ESCAPE\x0a\x0aA plugin SHALL not be able to escape its approved execution boundary.\x0a\x0aEscape attempts SHALL be treated as security events.\x0a\x0aWhere technically possible, LANUX SHALL isolate plugin execution from unrelated core components.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 77. PRIVILEGE ESCALATION\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL not self-elevate.\x0a\x0aRequests for elevated host privileges SHALL pass through host installation and authorization mechanisms.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 78. PLUGIN-TO-PLUGIN ACCESS\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL not automatically access other plugins.\x0a\x0aInter-plugin communication SHALL be explicit and governed.\x0a\x0aShared data SHALL have defined ownership and classification.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 79. PLUGIN DEPENDENCY TRUST\x0a\x0aA plugin dependency SHALL not inherit the parent plugin's authority automatically.\x0a\x0aEach dependency SHALL have its own:\x0a\x0a- identity\x3b\x0a- provenance\x3b\x0a- integrity\x3b\x0a- compatibility\x3b\x0a- trust\x3b\x0a- capability requirements.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 80. PLUGIN DISCOVERY BY MODELS\x0a\x0aModels MAY propose plugins based on task requirements.\x0a\x0aModel recommendations SHALL remain recommendations until authorized.\x0a\x0aA model SHALL not install or activate a plugin merely because it determines that the plugin would be useful.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 81. PLUGIN DISCOVERY BY AUTONOMOUS TASKS\x0a\x0aAutonomous tasks MAY discover plugins only within approved discovery scopes.\x0a\x0aDiscovery SHALL not imply installation authority.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 82. PLUGIN INSTALLATION FROM EXTERNAL INSTRUCTIONS\x0a\x0aInstructions from websites, files, messages, providers, or plugins SHALL not automatically authorize installation.\x0a\x0aThe creator or governed policy SHALL approve installation according to risk.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 83. BROWSER-DOWNLOADED PLUGINS\x0a\x0aPlugins downloaded through browser automation SHALL be treated as externally sourced packages.\x0a\x0aThey SHALL undergo the same validation, integrity, compatibility, and authorization process as other packages.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 84. LOCAL PACKAGE IMPORT\x0a\x0aLocal plugin packages SHALL be treated as untrusted until validated.\x0a\x0aUSB-local does not automatically mean trusted.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 85. PLUGIN REMOVAL\x0a\x0aRemoval SHALL support:\x0a\x0a- disable\x3b\x0a- quarantine\x3b\x0a- revoke\x3b\x0a- uninstall\x3b\x0a- cleanup\x3b\x0a- rollback.\x0a\x0aEach action SHALL have distinct semantics.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 86. REMOVAL FAILURE\x0a\x0aIf uninstall partially fails, LANUX SHALL preserve an accurate ownership and remaining-state record.\x0a\x0aIt SHALL not claim complete removal unless verification confirms it.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 87. RECOVERY\x0a\x0aRecovery SHALL be able to restore or remove plugins according to known-good state.\x0a\x0aRecovery SHALL validate:\x0a\x0a- package integrity\x3b\x0a- version\x3b\x0a- dependencies\x3b\x0a- configuration\x3b\x0a- permissions\x3b\x0a- state\x3b\x0a- registration.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 88. USB REMOVAL\x0a\x0aPlugin activity SHALL integrate with USB Removal & Safe Shutdown.\x0a\x0aUSB removal SHALL stop new plugin work and safely handle plugin child processes.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 89. SHUTDOWN\x0a\x0aPlugin shutdown SHALL support:\x0a\x0a- quiescing\x3b\x0a- cancellation\x3b\x0a- child-process termination\x3b\x0a- state persistence\x3b\x0a- resource release\x3b\x0a- log flush\x3b\x0a- event handling.\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL not prevent LANUX from reaching safe shutdown indefinitely.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 90. UPDATE INTERRUPTION\x0a\x0aPlugin updates SHALL tolerate:\x0a\x0a- USB removal\x3b\x0a- power loss\x3b\x0a- process crash\x3b\x0a- host restart\x3b\x0a- network interruption.\x0a\x0aStaged transactions SHALL permit recovery or rollback.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 91. CONCURRENCY\x0a\x0aConcurrent plugin lifecycle operations SHALL be serialized where required.\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL prevent conflicting operations such as:\x0a\x0a- uninstall during activation\x3b\x0a- update during rollback\x3b\x0a- revoke during privileged execution\x3b\x0a- dependency removal while still required.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 92. PLUGIN LOCKS\x0a\x0aPlugin state SHALL use controlled locking where concurrent access is possible.\x0a\x0aLocks SHALL not create permanent deadlocks.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 93. EVENT PROPAGATION\x0a\x0aPlugin lifecycle and execution events SHALL integrate with the Event Bus & State Propagation Contract.\x0a\x0aEvents SHALL preserve plugin provenance.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 94. EVENT AUTHORITY\x0a\x0aPlugin-generated events SHALL not automatically modify authoritative LANUX state.\x0a\x0aState transitions SHALL be applied by the responsible subsystem.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 95. PLUGIN DATA FLOW\x0a\x0aThe architecture SHALL document data flow where plugins:\x0a\x0a- receive creator input\x3b\x0a- receive model output\x3b\x0a- receive task context\x3b\x0a- access files\x3b\x0a- access network data\x3b\x0a- access secrets\x3b\x0a- invoke tools\x3b\x0a- produce external side effects.\x0a\x0aData flow SHALL respect classification and minimization.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 96. PRIVACY\x0a\x0aPlugin data collection SHALL be limited to approved purpose.\x0a\x0aPlugins SHALL not silently collect unrelated creator, host, task, memory, or file data.\x0a\x0aExternal transmission SHALL be governed by network and privacy contracts.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 97. TELEMETRY\x0a\x0aPlugin telemetry SHALL be:\x0a\x0a- bounded\x3b\x0a- classified\x3b\x0a- privacy-aware\x3b\x0a- redacted\x3b\x0a- resource-limited.\x0a\x0aTelemetry SHALL not contain secret values.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 98. EMERGENCY DISABLEMENT\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL support emergency disablement of plugin execution.\x0a\x0aEmergency disablement MAY be triggered by:\x0a\x0a- security incident\x3b\x0a- resource exhaustion\x3b\x0a- repeated crash\x3b\x0a- provider compromise\x3b\x0a- creator command\x3b\x0a- system security hold.\x0a\x0aEmergency disablement SHALL not delete evidence required for investigation or recovery.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 99. PLUGIN INVENTORY\x0a\x0aLANUX SHALL maintain an authoritative inventory of plugin state.\x0a\x0aInventory SHOULD include:\x0a\x0a- plugin identity\x3b\x0a- version\x3b\x0a- source\x3b\x0a- publisher\x3b\x0a- installation state\x3b\x0a- activation state\x3b\x0a- permissions\x3b\x0a- dependencies\x3b\x0a- health\x3b\x0a- provenance\x3b\x0a- ownership\x3b\x0a- configuration\x3b\x0a- update state\x3b\x0a- revocation state.\x0a\x0aInventory SHALL remain persistent where required.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 100. MULTI-HOST BEHAVIOR\x0a\x0aPlugin inventory SHALL distinguish portable plugin state from host-specific activation.\x0a\x0aA plugin installed or enabled on one host SHALL not automatically be assumed active on another host.\x0a\x0aHost-specific requirements SHALL be re-evaluated.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 101. REQUIREMENTS GATE\x0a\x0aBefore production plugin architecture is implemented, LANUX SHALL establish from the completed repository:\x0a\x0a- existing ToolRegistry plugin support\x3b\x0a- existing integration mechanisms\x3b\x0a- existing package/runtime management\x3b\x0a- existing PermissionGateway behavior\x3b\x0a- existing SecretsVault behavior\x3b\x0a- existing Sandbox boundaries\x3b\x0a- existing task/model integration\x3b\x0a- existing event architecture\x3b\x0a- existing configuration architecture\x3b\x0a- existing update/recovery mechanisms\x3b\x0a- existing tests\x3b\x0a- actual runtime architecture\x3b\x0a- actual host integration requirements.\x0a\x0aProduction implementation SHALL begin only after these requirements are verified.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 102. IMPLEMENTATION BOUNDARY\x0a\x0aThis contract authorizes architecture definition only.\x0a\x0aIt does NOT authorize:\x0a\x0a- modifying the LANUX development repository\x3b\x0a- modifying ToolRegistry\x3b\x0a- modifying PermissionGateway\x3b\x0a- modifying SecretsVault\x3b\x0a- modifying Sandbox\x3b\x0a- modifying TaskStore\x3b\x0a- modifying ProjectStore\x3b\x0a- modifying model orchestration\x3b\x0a- modifying event architecture\x3b\x0a- modifying runtime files\x3b\x0a- modifying USB launchers\x3b\x0a- modifying host installation logic\x3b\x0a- modifying NUKRAX web files\x3b\x0a- modifying recovery media.\x0a\x0aAny future implementation SHALL first inspect the completed LANUX repository and existing tests.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 103. SOURCE OF TRUTH\x0a\x0aThe completed LANUX repository remains authoritative for actual plugin, extension, connector, integration, runtime, tool, permission, credential, task, model, and host implementation details.\x0a\x0aThis contract defines the portable USB architectural requirements and boundaries.\x0a\x0aWhere this contract differs from the completed implementation, the discrepancy SHALL be explicitly identified and resolved before production implementation.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 104. FINAL ACCEPTANCE CONDITION\x0a\x0aThe Plugin, Extension & External Integration architecture SHALL be considered complete only when:\x0a\x0a- plugin identity is defined\x3b\x0a- manifests are validated\x3b\x0a- provenance is preserved\x3b\x0a- source trust is defined\x3b\x0a- package integrity is verified\x3b\x0a- compatibility is checked\x3b\x0a- dependencies are governed\x3b\x0a- installation is staged and authorized\x3b\x0a- activation is controlled\x3b\x0a- lifecycle states are explicit\x3b\x0a- disablement and quarantine are supported\x3b\x0a- revocation is supported\x3b\x0a- uninstall is ownership-aware\x3b\x0a- host modifications are governed\x3b\x0a- filesystem and process access are scoped\x3b\x0a- network and browser access are scoped\x3b\x0a- SecretsVault remains the credential authority\x3b\x0a- plugin secret access is bounded\x3b\x0a- creator authorization remains distinct\x3b\x0a- session and task boundaries are preserved\x3b\x0a- model-generated plugin actions remain governed\x3b\x0a- ToolRegistry remains authoritative for registered capabilities\x3b\x0a- PermissionGateway remains authoritative for authorization\x3b\x0a- plugin events remain governed\x3b\x0a- UI integration cannot bypass backend authority\x3b\x0a- resource usage is bounded\x3b\x0a- health and diagnostics are observable\x3b\x0a- logging and audit are integrated\x3b\x0a- updates and rollback are controlled\x3b\x0a- supply-chain risks are addressed\x3b\x0a- malicious behavior blast radius is bounded\x3b\x0a- recovery behavior is defined\x3b\x0a- USB removal and shutdown behavior are defined\x3b\x0a- offline behavior is defined\x3b\x0a- multi-host behavior is defined\x3b\x0a- plugin inventory is authoritative\x3b\x0a- emergency disablement is available\x3b\x0a- testing requirements are established\x3b\x0a- production implementation remains gated until final LANUX requirements are verified.\x0a\x0aUntil these conditions are satisfied, production plugin and external integration implementation SHALL remain gated.\x0a\x0a---\x0a\x0a## 105. STATUS\x0a\x0aContract: PLUGIN, EXTENSION & EXTERNAL INTEGRATION CONTRACT\x0aNumber: 24\x0aStatus: LOCKED\x0aImplementation: NOT AUTHORIZED BY THIS CONTRACT\x0aProduction implementation: GATED ON FINAL LANUX REQUIREMENTS\x0a\x0a'@ | Set-Content -LiteralPath $Path -Encoding UTF8\x0a\x0aGet-Item -LiteralPath $Path | Select-Object FullName, Length, LastWriteTime;ef5fddfa-7684-4d37-8220-d78e4665db4e
